{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310679,"nonce":"f0XEKs3h1xkRa5mjjfG6JafAGt0=","title":"Q4_K_M 2-row SDOT tile attempt failed isolated A/B (+0.01% median, p=0.82) on Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M decode. M1 Pro Q4_K_M decode path is ...","message":"Attempted mirror of the proven Q6_K 2-row SDOT tile pattern (commit 6285c22) onto the Q4_K_M SDOT dot path in `runtime/src/quant.rs::qmatmul_chunk_impl`. Per the workflow ranking in [[perf_optimization_workflow_session_2026_06]] this was the #1 surviving candidate after adversarial-verify of 36 candidates (6 confirmed, 30 rejected), with surveyor estimate \"+2-5% wall, 30% chance net-zero\".\n\n**Why it should have won:** Q4_K_M lm_head matmul (vocab=128256 rows × hidden=2048) is now dominant on every decode token after the F32→Q4_K_M re-quant landed at 7394cba/36a2f8b. Two consecutive weight rows × one shared `x_q8` quant block = two independent SDOT accumulator chains amortising x→Q8 reconstruction across two output rows. This is the exact pattern that earlier amortised Q6_K's per-block reconstruction cost.\n\n**Why it didn't:** Q4_K_M single-row SDOT was already well-optimised at 144 B/block (vs Q6_K's 210 B/block). The bandwidth headroom Q6_K's tile bought doesn't exist for Q4_K_M — and the tile's only mechanism is bandwidth amortisation, not compute reduction. The CPU's HW prefetcher already keeps consecutive 144-byte weight rows hot; the tile just adds outer-loop bookkeeping for the same compute.\n\n**Methodology (this is the right discipline; reuse for future kernel A/Bs):**\n- Same binary, same .rayzor cache, env-flag gate `RAYZOR_USE_Q4KM_TILE2`\n- Alternating ON/OFF/ON/OFF for N=20 pairs (40 runs total)\n- Cold scrub once at start; warmup = 2 runs discarded\n- 64-token decode of the Paris-history prompt at temp=0.01 with greedy sampler\n- Paired sign test on (ON - OFF) deltas\n\nThis avoids the \"+13.8% naive / -4.7% real\" confound bugs_q6k_sdot_no_win.md documents from naive `git stash` A/B.\n\n**Results (Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, M1 Pro, AC):**\n- median(ON)  = 20.782 tok/s\n- median(OFF) = 20.780 tok/s\n- **+0.01% median** wall-time\n- paired median delta = -0.047 tok/s (slight negative)\n- sign test: ON \u003e OFF in 9/20 pairs, p = 0.82 (no effect detected)\n- outlier-trimmed (drop |delta|≥2.0): 8/19, median delta -0.083\n\n**Conclusion:** Reverted (no measurable win, mild risk of negative). M1 Pro Q4_K_M decode path is performance-saturated in this regime — bandwidth amortisation tricks that worked on Q6_K don't transfer because Q4_K_M's block is 31% smaller. Per [[project_optimization_roadmap]] tier-1 candidates: further per-token gains on M1 Pro require either (a) Q8_0 KV cache (blocked on Haxe JIT layout-shift bug — bugs_q8_kv_cache_attempted.md), (b) fixing the decode-loop per-step leak (bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak.md), or (c) moving to M2+ for ARMv8.6 SMMLA / i8mm (`sysctl hw.optional.arm.FEAT_I8MM = 0` on this hardware).\n\nThe bench harness is preserved at `/tmp/q4km_tile_bench.sh` for re-use against future kernel-tile experiments (Q4_K_M SwiGLU fused matmul, etc.). The alternating-pair sign-test pattern is the reusable discipline.\n\n**Useful side effect:** The disciplined null result re-validates the [[perf_flash_attn_decode]] +40.7% number from 7835a26 — that win held under the same isolation methodology, this one didn't. The methodology itself is calibrated.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_q4km_sdot_tile_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}